# Docker Compose companion to .env.example (Story 6.2).
# Copy to ".env.docker" and fill in the REAL value — it is git-ignored like ".env".
#
# Why this file exists: MEDILABO_PASSWORD_BCRYPT contains literal "$" characters
# (a BCrypt hash, e.g. $2a$10$...). Docker Compose re-interpolates "$" in any value
# it substitutes from ".env", silently corrupting the hash. Properties files (read
# directly by Spring Boot via spring.config.import) have no such escaping rule, so
# the same literal value cannot live in both files. This file uses Compose's "$$"
# escape and is wired into docker-compose.yml via env_file: — Spring Boot never
# reads it.
MEDILABO_PASSWORD_BCRYPT=$$2a$$10$$replace-with-a-real-bcrypt-hash
